<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Zaydun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1003–<a href="/years/1071">1071</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Zaydun (1003–1071) was an Arab poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, regarded as one of the greatest poets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He served the Jahwarid government of Córdoba but was imprisoned and fled, and later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to the Abbadid kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, al-Mutadid and al-Mutamid. His love poems to the Umayyad princess Wallada bint al-Mustakfi and his literary epistles are classics of Arabic literature.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abū al-Walīd Aḥmad Ibn Zaydouni al-Makhzūmī, known as Ibn Zaydun, was born in 1003 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> into an aristocratic Andalusian Arab family descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-makhzum">Banu Makhzum</a>. He grew up during the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of Córdoba and was involved in the political life of his age. He joined the court of the Jahwarid Abu al-Hazm of Córdoba, but was imprisoned by him after accusations that he had conspired against him and his patrons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ibn Zaydun's romantic and literary life was dominated by his relationship with the poet and Umayyad princess Wallada bint al-Mustakfi, daughter of the Umayyad Caliph Muhammad III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Wallada herself quickly ended it. Some attributed her change of heart to his early anti-Umayyad activities, while others mention his rivalry with the rich minister Ibn Abdus, a former friend, who supposedly won her favour and may himself have turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hazm-ibn-jahwar">Abu al-Hazm ibn Jahwar</a> against Ibn Zaydun.</p><p>He sought refuge with Abbad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and his son al-Mu'tamid, and was able to return home for a period after the ruler of Seville conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Much of his life was spent in exile. He died in 1071.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ibn Zaydun is considered the leading neoclassical poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He reinvigorated impassioned lyrics in Arabic by giving them a more personal and sensual tone, and came to be regarded as the best of the love poets of Muslim Hispania and a model for all later Western Arabic poetry. His love affair with Wallada and his exile inspired many of his poems, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of lost youth and nostalgia for his city recur in them. In a poem on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> he remembers it with the words: how many girls like images trailed their garments among such flowers, when life was fresh and time was at our service.</p><p>According to Jayyusi, in The Legacy of Muslim Spain, he brought into Andalusi poetry a sense of balance, rhetorical command and the passionate power and grandeur of style that marked contemporary poetry in the east, rescuing it from the self-indulgence of poets of externalised description.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibn Zaydun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poet <b>Ibn Zaydun</b> served the regime before falling out with it.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-cordoba">Taifa of Córdoba</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1047" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4431 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ibn Zaydun"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1047">What was here in 1047? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
